(
  'asian-koel','Asian Koel','कोकीळ','कोयल','Eudynamys scolopaceus','birds','songbirds','🐦',
  'The invisible herald of the Indian mango season — a brood parasite that lays its eggs in crow nests while the male calls koo-oo!',
  'Asian_koel',
  '{"kingdom":"Animalia","phylum":"Chordata","class":"Aves","order":"Cuculiformes","family":"Cuculidae","genus":"Eudynamys","species":"E. scolopaceus"}'::jsonb,
  'Linnaeus, 1758. Sits at the heart of Indian spring poetry.','LC','Common and widespread',
  'Length 39–46 cm; wingspan ~55 cm',44,0.24,'Frugivore',
  ARRAY['Figs','Papaya','Mango','Berries'],'Among the few large Asian birds dependent mainly on fruit; males ad lib sweet duets at dawn.',
  14,18,ARRAY['Deciduous forest','Urban','Farmland'],ARRAY['Asia','Australia & Oceania'],ARRAY['India','Bangladesh','Sri Lanka','China','Indonesia'],
  '[{"label":"Brood parasitism","value":"Lays in crow and myna nests"},{"label":"Clutch","value":"Single egg per host nest"},{"label":"Chick","value":"Hatches early and outcompetes host chicks"}]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"Call","value":"Piercing koo-oo rising in intensity each mango season"},{"label":"Sexual dimorphism","value":"Males glossy black, females spotted brown-white"},{"label":"Stealth","value":"Females sneak eggs while hosts are away"}]'::jsonb,
  'Diurnal','60 km/h',
  ARRAY['Host bird decline','Fruit-tree removal'],
  'Tolerate life in crow colonies; hosts and koels coevolve in balance.',
  ARRAY['Koels never build a nest of their own.','Only males sing the famous koo-oo; females make a clicking call.','A koel chick gets fed by crow parents many times its own size.','Mango flowering and the first koel calls arrive together in spring.'],
  ARRAY['house-crow','red-vented-bulbul','common-myna'],ARRAY['india','cuckoo','brood parasite','mango season'],68
),
(
  'house-sparrow','House Sparrow','चिमणी','गौरैया','Passer domesticus','birds','songbirds','🐦',
  'The little brown companion of human settlements for 10,000 years — now declining in cities and a poignant indicator of urban ecosystem health.',
  'House_sparrow',
  '{"kingdom":"Animalia","phylum":"Chordata","class":"Aves","order":"Passeriformes","family":"Passeridae","genus":"Passer","species":"P. domesticus"}'::jsonb,
  'Linnaeus, 1758. World Sparrow Day: 20 March.','LC','Trillions worldwide historically; urban numbers falling sharply',
  'Length 14–16 cm; wingspan 21–25 cm',15,0.03,'Omnivore',
  ARRAY['Grain','Grass seeds','Insects for chicks','Kitchen crumbs'],'Nestlings are raised almost entirely on insects, which is why pesticide-sprayed cities starve sparrows.',
  5,13,ARRAY['Urban','Farmland'],ARRAY['Asia','Europe','Africa','North America','Australia & Oceania'],ARRAY['India','Worldwide near humans'],
  '[{"label":"Clutch","value":"3–5 eggs, several broods/year"},{"label":"Nest","value":"Crevices in houses, nest boxes"},{"label":"Incubation","value":"10–14 days"}]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"Bond with humans","value":"Followed agriculture across every continent"},{"label":"Bathing","value":"Dust baths to maintain plumage"},{"label":"Colonial","value":"Communal roosts chatter until dark"}]'::jsonb,
  'Diurnal','40 km/h',
  ARRAY['Loss of insects','Modern sealed buildings','Glass infertility? no — habitat change'],
  'India placed the sparrow as a state bird of Bihar and Delhi; nest-box and grain schemes are restoring colonies.',
  ARRAY['World Sparrow Day is on 20 March, started in Nashik, India.','The sparrow is the state bird of Delhi and Bihar.','It has lived besides humans for about 10,000 years.','Chicks die without insect protein, even on unlimited grain.'],
  ARRAY['baya-weaver','house-crow','red-vented-bulbul'],ARRAY['urban','indicator','declining','nest boxes'],67
),
(
  'common-myna','Common Myna','मैना','मैना','Acridotheres tristis','birds','songbirds','🐦',
